{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"reviews\":[{\"id\":\"structural_monotony\",\"verdict\":\"insufficient_evidence\",\"rationale\":\"The judgment is plausible because the feature sections share a heading-plus-explanation-benefit pattern, but the annotator supplied no multi-part evidence for a whole-document structural claim.\",\"evidence\":[\"- Deprioritize least commonly used features: We reviewed areas that we felt could be delayed and delivered on “painting” the page faster so you can browse quicker.\",\"- Suspend Idle Tabs: You shouldn’t feel guilty about opening a zillion tabs, but keeping all those tabs open uses your computer’s memory and slows down its performance.\",\"- Faster startup after customization: For users who have customized their browser with an add-on like a favorite theme, for example changing it to the seasons of the year, or utilizing one of the popular ad-blockers, we’ve made it so that the browser skips a bunch of unnecessary work during subsequent start-ups.\"]},{\"id\":\"tonal_uniformity\",\"verdict\":\"undercall\",\"rationale\":\"The register largely holds as a single friendly corporate-explanatory voice. A colloquial phrase like \\\"a zillion tabs\\\" does not amount to a genuine register break.\",\"evidence\":[\"To make Firefox faster, we simply prioritized our performance management “to-do” list.\",\"For this latest Firefox release, we adopted the well-known time management strategy of “procrastinate on purpose.”\",\"You shouldn’t feel guilty about opening a zillion tabs, but keeping all those tabs open uses your computer’s memory and slows down its performance.\"]},{\"id\":\"faux_specificity\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"Both flagged phrases simulate concreteness through familiar lifestyle detail without supplying lived, document-specific grounding.\",\"evidence\":[\"before you go on a road trip, you check for a full tank of gas, make sure you have enough oil, or have the right air pressure in your tires.\",\"changing it to the seasons of the year\"]},{\"id\":\"neutrality_collapse\",\"verdict\":\"insufficient_evidence\",\"rationale\":\"The document clearly argues that the release made Firefox faster rather than flattening into balance, but the annotator gave no textual support for this whole-document stance judgment.\",\"evidence\":[\"To make Firefox faster, we simply prioritized our performance management “to-do” list.\",\"The result is that Firefox is better at performing tasks at the optimal time.\",\"Here’s how we reorganized our to-do list to make Firefox faster:\"]},{\"id\":\"even_jargon_distribution\",\"verdict\":\"insufficient_evidence\",\"rationale\":\"The conclusion is plausible because technical vocabulary clusters in the feature bullets, but the annotator did not supply the cross-document evidence needed for a distribution judgment.\",\"evidence\":[\"We applied many of the same principles of time management just like you might prioritize your own urgent needs.\",\"This includes delaying setTimeout in order to prioritize scripts\",\"scanning for alternative style sheets after page load; and not loading the auto-fill module unless there is an actual form to complete.\"]},{\"id\":\"forced_synesthesia\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The document uses analogy and marketing prose, but it does not use cross-modal sensory mappings of the kind this record targets.\",\"evidence\":[\"performance management “to-do” list\",\"procrastinate on purpose\",\"opening a zillion tabs\"]},{\"id\":\"generic_metaphors\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The flagged metaphor is a familiar productivity frame that substitutes for a more concrete account of engineering prioritization.\",\"evidence\":[\"our performance management “to-do” list\"]},{\"id\":\"referential_clarity\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The document has some vague wording, but it does not materially rely on unclear pronoun reference or unsupported first-use definites.\",\"evidence\":[\"The result is that Firefox is better at performing tasks at the optimal time.\",\"This includes delaying setTimeout in order to prioritize scripts\",\"simply click on the tab, and it will reload where you left off.\"]},{\"id\":\"formulaic_parallelism\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The road-trip sentence uses a balanced three-part checklist whose symmetry does more rhetorical than informational work for the Firefox explanation.\",\"evidence\":[\"before you go on a road trip, you check for a full tank of gas, make sure you have enough oil, or have the right air pressure in your tires.\"]},{\"id\":\"semantic_redundancy\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"No nearby sentence merely restates an earlier claim or list item; the short piece stays additive rather than repetitious at the sentence level.\",\"evidence\":[\"The result is that Firefox is better at performing tasks at the optimal time.\",\"Here’s how we reorganized our to-do list to make Firefox faster:\",\"Rest assured if you decide you want to review that webpage, simply click on the tab, and it will reload where you left off.\"]},{\"id\":\"underspecified_language\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"Each flagged phrase leaves out the relevant metric or criterion needed to make the claim precise.\",\"evidence\":[\"least commonly used features\",\"browse quicker\",\"unnecessary work\"]},{\"id\":\"context_leakage\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The prose does not answer an absent critic or depend on missing chat context; it reads as a self-contained product explainer.\",\"evidence\":[\"To make Firefox faster, we simply prioritized our performance management “to-do” list.\",\"Here’s how we reorganized our to-do list to make Firefox faster:\"]},{\"id\":\"performed_candour\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The document does not use sincerity-marking adverbs or authenticity boosters such as \\\"honestly\\\" or \\\"frankly.\\\"\",\"evidence\":[\"To make Firefox faster, we simply prioritized our performance management “to-do” list.\",\"For this latest Firefox release, we adopted the well-known time management strategy of “procrastinate on purpose.”\"]},{\"id\":\"vacuous_connection\",\"verdict\":\"supported\",\"rationale\":\"The connectors in the piece state recognizable relationships rather than implying an unstated one.\",\"evidence\":[\"The result is that Firefox is better at performing tasks at the optimal time.\",\"but keeping all those tabs open uses your computer’s memory and slows down its performance.\"]},{\"id\":\"genre_specific\",\"verdict\":\"misclassified\",\"rationale\":\"This reads as journalism-style product reporting rather than `default`, and under the journalism watchlist it has unsupported performance claims with no named source or link.\",\"evidence\":[\"# How did we make Firefox faster?\",\"the main scripts for Instagram, Amazon and Google searches execute 40-80% faster;\",\"Firefox will now detect if your computer’s memory is running low, which we define as lower than 400MB,\"]}]}"}}
